  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMid-level trough will continue to tracking east-northeast \nacross the northern Appalachians through this morning. A\nconveyor belt of deep moisture with PWAT's of 1.75-2\" combined\nwith deep warm cloud layers will be favorable for efficient \nwarm rain processes, allowing for areas of moderate to locally \nheavy rain across much of the region through daybreak, shifting \nto areas mainly from the Capital Region and areas south/east \nthrough mid morning. Rainfall rates of 0.25-0.50\"/hour are \nexpected, with locally higher rates within any deeper embedded \nconvective elements and/or narrow banding that develops. Latest \nCAMs suggest that areas near the I-90 corridor may have one \nmaxima of rainfall associated with mid level F-Gen forcing, \nwhile areas farther southeast across the mid Hudson Valley and \nNW CT may have a second maxima where deeper convective elements \nand greater low level forcing closer to a developing surface \nwarm/coastal front occur.\n\n00Z/13 HREF 3-hour probs for >3\" rainfall at 10-30% across\nportions of the mid Hudson Valley and NW CT between 8-11 AM this\nmorning, with similar probs for >1\" at 50-70% or higher as far\nnorth as portions of the Capital Region, Berkshires and extreme\nsouthern VT, and these are the areas where ponding of water in\nlow lying/poor drainage and urban areas is more probable. There\nis also a low probability for isolated flash flooding in some of\nthese areas. Heavy rain should shift east of these areas by late\nthis morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nIn the wake of this morning's rainfall, generally dry conditions\nare then expected from late morning through at least mid\nafternoon, despite mostly cloudy skies. Some breaks in the\nclouds are expected to eventually develop by late afternoon. An\napproaching cold front is then expected to track into western\nand central NY later this afternoon, triggering isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms. Latest HREFs suggest MU\nCAPE of 750-1250 J/kg develops later today, although is highly\ndependent on how much sunshine can break through. At this time,\nat least isolated to scattered thunderstorms are expected to\nreach the Mohawk Valley and SW Adirondacks around sunset, which\nshould then track east/southeast toward the Capital Region and\nLake George/Saratoga region shortly thereafter. Despite waning\ninstability, strengthening mid level winds and approaching upper\nlevel support could sustain strong thunderstorms for a few hours\neven after sunset, should then materialize. A few instances of\ndamaging wind gusts and/or large hail will be possible. The\nlatest Day 1 convective outlook from SPC places most of eastern\nNY and southern VT within a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for\nsevere thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA much cooler air mass will overspread the region later tonight\ninto Monday in the wake of the cold front. It will be breezy\nMonday with northwest winds gusting 20-30 mph at times. High\npressure will then build overhead for Monday night, providing\nexcellent radiational cooling conditions. Low temps by Tue AM\nshould fall into the 40s for most areas, except 30s across much\nof the southern Adirondacks and upper Hudson Valley. Patchy\nfrost will be possible across the southern Adirondacks, and will\nneed to monitor in case the potential for more widespread frost\npotential necessitates frost advisory issuances for these areas.",
